1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,900
(Transcribed by TurboScribe. Go Unlimited to remove this message.) It's almost 1am, you're replaying something you said

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:06,740
9 hours ago, and you cannot stop turning

3
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:07,220
it over.

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,840
Everyone calls this overthinking.

5
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:11,720
Like, it's a flaw.

6
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:13,020
Isn't it?

7
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:16,400
Most people treat overthinking like a personal weakness,

8
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:19,540
something broken, something to medicate away.

9
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,460
But here's what's strange.

10
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:25,180
In 2015, a group of researchers in Canada

11
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:28,420
sat down with over 100 college students.

12
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:32,120
They measured how verbal each student was, how

13
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:34,100
quickly they could reason with words.

14
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:37,240
The sharper their verbal mind, the more they

15
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:37,580
worried.

16
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:40,980
The more they replayed conversations in their head,

17
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:43,020
not less, more.

18
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:45,660
The same trait that made them quick with

19
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:48,620
words made them slower to let a thought

20
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:48,920
go.

21
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,800
Which raises an uncomfortable question.

22
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,940
What if overthinking and intelligence aren't two different

23
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:56,340
things?

24
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,540
What if they are the same machine, running

25
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:02,480
the exact same program, just pointed in two

26
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:03,360
different directions?

27
00:01:04,019 --> 00:01:07,700
24 centuries ago, in Athens, a question was

28
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:10,000
written down that nobody could answer.

29
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:14,460
Why is it, the text asked, that everyone

30
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:18,580
who becomes truly exceptional in philosophy or poetry

31
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:22,000
or politics turns out to be a melancholic?

32
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,200
Not some of them, everyone.

33
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:28,400
The text is called the Problemata, likely written

34
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,620
by a student of Aristotle's.

35
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,240
And it doesn't speak in vague terms, it

36
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:34,500
names names.

37
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:38,600
Socrates, Plato, Empedocles.

38
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:42,920
The sharpest minds Greece had ever produced, all

39
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:46,940
of them restless, unsettled, turning inward.

40
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:51,820
Seneca, writing later, put Aristotle's own view even

41
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:52,960
more bluntly.

42
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:56,740
No great intellect, he wrote, exists without some

43
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:57,860
mixture of madness.

44
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,600
That idea didn't fade with the Greeks.

45
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:05,260
1768, a Swiss physician named Tissot sits down

46
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:07,639
to write the first medical book about a

47
00:02:07,639 --> 00:02:09,380
very specific kind of patient.

48
00:02:09,759 --> 00:02:13,340
Not soldiers, not laborers, scholars.

49
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:15,940
Men who read and wrote for a living.

50
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:20,240
Tissot documented them one by one, exhausted, depleted,

51
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:22,040
undone by their own minds.

52
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:25,920
He gave it a name, Literary Intemperance.

53
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:29,580
Thinking too much, diagnosed as an occupational disease,

54
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:33,020
200 years before anyone said the word burnout.

55
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,700
So why does this keep happening across centuries,

56
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:37,680
to the sharpest minds specifically?

57
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:40,360
Researchers have a name for the ability they

58
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:41,400
are all overusing.

59
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,920
They call it mental time travel.

60
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:47,000
It's the skill that lets you leave the

61
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,680
present moment and walk through a future that

62
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:50,760
hasn't happened yet.

63
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,200
Plan for it, rehearse it, feel it before

64
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:54,760
it's real.

65
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:58,140
For most of human history, that skill kept

66
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:58,980
people alive.

67
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:02,000
See the danger before it arrives, prepare for

68
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,360
the winter before it's cold.

69
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,800
But the same machine that stimulates a good

70
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:10,140
future can just as easily stimulate a disaster

71
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:11,280
that never comes.

72
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:15,160
Researchers studying this call it a threat detection

73
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:15,800
bias.

74
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,080
The mind doesn't just imagine what could go

75
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:22,460
right, it specializes in finding what could go

76
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:22,980
wrong.

77
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:26,680
And once it starts looking, it doesn't want

78
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:27,220
to stop.

79
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:29,880
Here's where the brain scans get involved.

80
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:33,740
In 2012, researchers at a New York medical

81
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:37,320
school put 44 people through brain imaging.

82
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,240
Half had a diagnosed anxiety disorder, half didn't.

83
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:45,220
They measured IQ, they measured worry, and they

84
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,900
scanned something deep in the brain's white matter,

85
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:49,840
a chemical called choline.

86
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:52,760
And the same pattern in that tissue predicted

87
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:56,260
both things, higher intelligence and higher worry.

88
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,400
Not as two separate traits sitting next to

89
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:02,240
each other, as one signature in the same

90
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:03,760
place, in the same brain.

91
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:06,700
The researchers had a striking way of putting

92
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:06,980
it.

93
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:10,240
Intelligence, they wrote, may have co-evolved with

94
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:10,540
worry.

95
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,320
Not as a side effect, as a package

96
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:13,660
deal.

97
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:16,200
And it shows up outside the lab too.

98
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,100
A researcher at a small college once surveyed

99
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,520
over 3,000 members of MENISA, the society

100
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,500
for people who scored in the top 2

101
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:25,500
% for IQ.

102
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,120
She asked them about their mental health.

103
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:32,380
Anxiety disorders showed up at roughly twice the

104
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:33,200
national rate.

105
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:35,600
Not higher, twice.

106
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,760
She called it the hyperbrain hyperbody theory.

107
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:42,740
A nervous system that runs hot in one

108
00:04:42,740 --> 00:04:45,940
direction tends to run hot in every direction.

109
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,680
The same sensitivity that notices what everyone else

110
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,960
misses, the pattern, the subtext, the thing about

111
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:55,880
to go wrong, comes with the cost attached.

112
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,520
So put the pieces next to each other.

113
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:02,720
Verbal intelligence predicting more rumination, not less.

114
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:06,480
Brain tissue carrying one signature for both intelligence

115
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:07,240
and worry.

116
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:11,360
A society of the measurably gifted anxious at

117
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:12,980
twice the average rate.

118
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:14,980
That's not three coincidences.

119
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:18,900
That's one finding showing up three different ways.

120
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:22,060
Your mind doesn't have a separate switch for

121
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:24,780
thinking clearly and a separate switch for worrying

122
00:05:24,780 --> 00:05:25,400
too much.

123
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:26,920
It's the same switch.

124
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:29,460
The thing that makes you good at solving

125
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:32,540
problems is the same thing that won't let

126
00:05:32,540 --> 00:05:35,120
a problem go, once you have found one.

127
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:38,040
So here's what I keep thinking about.

128
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:41,400
That 1am replay, the conversation you can't put

129
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:44,540
down, it isn't proof that something's wrong with

130
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:44,980
your mind.

131
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:48,540
It's proof that the same mind's sharp enough

132
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:51,960
to notice everything doesn't come with an off

133
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:52,940
switch for noticing.

134
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:58,320
2400 years ago, someone watched the smartest people

135
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:01,520
they knew and wondered why they all seemed

136
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:02,580
so unsettled.

137
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:07,360
250 years ago, a doctor watched scholars burn

138
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:10,020
out from their own thinking and gave it

139
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:10,340
a name.

140
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:14,920
And tonight, it's you, still turning something over

141
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:17,940
and still running the stimulation one more time.

142
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:19,500
You're not broken.

143
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,000
You are just running the oldest, most expensive

144
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,440
feature the human mind has ever built.

145
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:28,460
The same one that lets you plan and

146
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:32,320
predict and prepare just occasionally pointed at 3am

147
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:33,340
instead of tomorrow.

148
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:36,320
So maybe tonight, let the thought finish its

149
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,460
lap and then let it go.

150
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:41,220
Not because you're wrong to think so much,

151
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,000
but because the mind that got the problem

152
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,380
already did its job.

153
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:48,020
You don't need to silence the thinking.

154
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:50,340
You just need to stop calling it broken.

155
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:54,840
Because somewhere in Athens 24 centuries ago, someone

156
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:58,220
was asking why the brightest minds couldn't rest.

157
00:06:58,220 --> 00:07:02,620
In Geneva, 250 years ago, a doctor was

158
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:05,920
writing the same question down in different language.

159
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:09,180
And tonight, at 1am, so are you.

160
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:12,360
Not a coincidence, not a flaw passed down

161
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:16,260
by accident, a thread running through every century

162
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:20,000
that ever produced someone who noticed too much

163
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,660
and couldn't look away.

164
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:25,160
So next time, the thought won't let go.

165
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:26,980
Try this instead of fighting.

166
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:31,080
Just notice what it's made of, the same

167
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:34,520
sharpness, the same depth, the same mind you'd

168
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:35,340
never trade away.

169
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:37,560
That's the whole point of tonight.

170
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:40,700
Not to think less, just to stop being

171
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:42,440
afraid of how much you think.

172
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,880
So tonight, when the thought comes back around,

173
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:49,420
let it just once more and then gently

174
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:50,640
let it rest.
